"The whole economy in one view. 13 sectors sit around Earth like slices of a pie. Distance from Earth is where the revenue-earning asset lives, from the ground out to deep space. Each slice has four floors: build, operate, apply and sell, enable. Colour shows how commercial a cell is in the chosen year.": "audio/v_1cf3836b17749bc4.mp3",
"Who is in the research, and where. Inside the atmosphere every company and agency sits at its headquarters, coloured by its main sector. Each country carries a spectrum column with one band per sector. The United States holds most of the research’s key players; China’s entries are mostly state actors. HQ locations are general knowledge, not yet cited.": "audio/v_3f62487094a86f44.mp3",
"The wider industry: the census. Beyond the research’s key players, a census from free public lists (agency registries, exhibitor lists, grant databases) counts 8,036 space companies. Each dot is one company, coloured by main sector. A filled dot sits in its headquarters city; a ring means no town could be found, so it sits in a sector-sorted grid at the country centre. In orbit, the same companies appear as small dots inside the cell where they work; those whose sources give no orbit sit in the dashed outer band. Scroll toward any spot to zoom in: names appear as you get close.": "audio/v_e73477e110fc2b72.mp3",
"Europe up close. France, Germany, Italy, Luxembourg and the UK each show a different spectrum: launch and satcom in France, manufacturing in Germany, servicing in Italy, satcom in Luxembourg.": "audio/v_285df3c7b6c372bb.mp3",
"Earth observation. Governments are the anchor customer here: estimates of their share run from about 43% to over 90% depending on who is asked. Every cell in this slice is agency-anchored (A), not yet fully commercial.": "audio/v_a70b62f58578d7ad.mp3",
"Operating constellations in LEO. The one EO cell with a measured market: about 3.8 billion dollars (2024, estimate). The beacon height shows that size on a log scale. Each green lane is one company’s constellation flowing through its orbit.": "audio/v_7683d2bfbad377c7.mp3",
"From headquarters to orbit. Planet builds its own satellites, operates them and sells analytics. The lines run from its San Francisco headquarters to every position it holds in the atlas.": "audio/v_97666952c241c0c1.mp3",
"SpaceX is everywhere. One company, positions in launch, satcom, defence, human spaceflight, lunar and more. Follow the lines from Starbase, Texas.": "audio/v_46e641122fad58f4.mp3",
"A gap: commercial EO from GEO. The amber frame marks a cell where sources show no commercial activity. GEO weather and carbon missions are run by agencies. A gap can be an opening or a sign the business does not close.": "audio/v_2c8fb66562f92d33.mp3",
"Unknown, not empty. Grey dashed cells have not been researched yet. High-altitude platforms for imaging are one. The atlas keeps “nobody is here” and “we have not looked” apart.": "audio/v_c1355fada19b1821.mp3",
"The ground under the sky. Every image has to come down. KSAT’s Svalbard station, the largest commercial ground facility, is marked at its real latitude on the globe.": "audio/v_6f904128ac6c7d60.mp3",
"Watch time move. The year steps from 2021 to 2025: WorldView Legion arrives. In 2030 and 2035 most cells turn dashed, because almost no source forecasts maturity that far out. The atlas shows forecasts only where one is cited.": "audio/v_c9bd86ba0a5fb49b.mp3",
"The real sky. The same Earth at true scale with every active satellite, 16,792 of them, moving on its real orbit. Coloured dots belong to companies in the research. Starlink alone is about two thirds of everything in orbit.": "audio/v_165d9f36273ab836.mp3",
"One fleet lit up. Planet’s Doves fly sun-synchronous polar orbits, crossing both poles on every pass. Use the fleet buttons in the panel to light up any operator, or click a single dot to see its orbit.": "audio/v_46f7bf5eb1fd5554.mp3",
"Finding 1. There is no single space-economy number. 2025 baselines differ by about 60% on definitions: SIA 429 billion dollars, Novaspace 626.4 billion dollars (core 236 billion dollars), Space Foundation 686 billion dollars. Never sum figures across publishers; treat over 1 trillion dollars forecasts as low confidence. The beacons show only cells with a measured segment total.": "audio/v_8542199c64e51058.mp3",
"Finding 2. Government is the anchor customer. Government space spending was about 138 to 141 billion dollars in 2025, the US over half. Buying shifted to fixed-price, multi-vendor services, so much “commercial” revenue is government money. That is why so many cells are A (agency-anchored), not C.": "audio/v_c6dc595abb9cd210.mp3",
"Finding 3. Launch is small in revenue but decisive. Launch revenue was 12.4 billion dollars in 2025, about 2.9% of SIA’s total, and one firm flies about half of global launches and over 80% of the mass.": "audio/v_d92b8ec52497aa4e.mp3",
"Finding 4. Satcom flipped from GEO TV to one LEO operator. Satellite services revenue fell from 110.2 billion dollars to 105.0 billion dollars (2023 to 2025) while Starlink reported about 11.4 billion dollars in 2025 (relayed from the S-1 only). In the real sky, Starlink is two thirds of all active satellites.": "audio/v_aa8bf71b2cd64615.mp3",
"Finding 5. The ground-equipment headline is mostly GNSS chips. Ground equipment was 142 billion dollars in 2021, about three quarters GNSS-related. KSAT, the largest commercial ground network, earned 2.36 billion Norwegian kroner. Split PNT out of GND or the model double-counts.": "audio/v_508e6440fe43c30d.mp3",
"Finding 6. The lunar economy is agency-anchored. Landed cost is about 1 million dollars per kilogram, and no commercial surface service had been demonstrated as of August 2024. Lunar oxygen sold in LEO loses to Falcon Heavy, so the propellant case does not close today.": "audio/v_f4398b210966e07a.mp3",
"Finding 7. Space resources have no measured revenue. The committed 2025 to 26 money is one 6.9 million dollars SBIR plus 12 to 15 million dollars seed rounds. Trillion-dollar asteroid values rest on a disowned method.": "audio/v_8284d12dc67d56dc.mp3",
"Finding 8. Software and AI are defensible only on an external moat. Owned data, qualification, scarce anomaly labels or government-programme lock-in. The generic layer is being absorbed by hyperscalers. Commercial space-situational awareness is the best-evidenced entry point.": "audio/v_07494515aad99fef.mp3",
"Terrarium in space. Lime marks where Terrarium creates value: 55 of 115 cells, grouped into 6 plays. Bright means sell now, darker means build with a partner, dashed means later. The tags mark the 8 strongest cells. This lens is our assessment for a pitch; the cells underneath stay the cited research.": "audio/v_d1e02986be29dd61.mp3",
"Earth answers too late. Light-speed delay, short contact windows, contested spectrum and export-controlled data push space companies to own their software and to change it fast without waiting for a vendor queue. In space, being dependent is not a cost problem, it is a mission risk.": "audio/v_ab0dd1a9b3cdf914.mp3",
"Play 1: Own the factory software layer. Sell now. Satellite, launcher and component builders are regulated mid-market manufacturers: exactly Terrarium's beachhead, and the largest group in the census. Replace the rented ERP, MES, quality and test-data tools with company-specific modules generated and run on the builder's own servers. Export-controlled design and test data (ITAR and EAR in the US) never leaves the building, and a change to the line takes a feature request, not a vendor project.": "audio/v_053b76dcd9a44c95.mp3",
"Play 2: Harden the ground stack. Sell now. The ground is the weak point: the KA-SAT attack came in through a misconfigured VPN. Licensing, ITU filings and supply-chain compliance are paperwork that every operator re-does by hand. Every generated module passes deterministic security gates and the operator, not a vendor, controls patching. Licensing, filing and supplier-compliance workflows become owned software that changes when the rules change.": "audio/v_cbfc9b6ec0672728.mp3",
"Play 3: Own mission and ground operations. Next. Ground systems and operations-as-a-service are growing, but operators rent the system of record and wait in vendor queues to change it. Contact scheduling, telemetry pipelines, anomaly triage and operations dashboards as modules the operator owns and can change overnight. A bug fix costs about 40k tokens and a feature about 400k, against about 5M for a rebuild.": "audio/v_e4ddc13fb99d4568.mp3",
"Play 4: Sovereign space-data processing. Next. Defence and intelligence are the largest buyers of Earth observation, and data-residency rules keep imagery, RF and GNSS data in the country. Imagery, RF and interference pipelines and the analyst apps on top, generated and run inside the customer's perimeter. The data and the code that processes it both stay under the customer's control.": "audio/v_2ce8e45a951272a6.mp3",
"Play 5: Own the traffic layer. Next. Orbits are getting crowded, and the research's finding 8 names commercial space-situational awareness as the best-evidenced software entry point. Conjunction screening, tracking-data fusion, manoeuvre planning and collision evidence as tools the operator or the state owns, instead of decisions made inside a shared multi-tenant platform.": "audio/v_7d376f48ebfcd78c.mp3",
"Play 6: Onboard autonomy software. Later. Earth answers too late: delay, contested links and radiation make onboard decisions table stakes for constellations, stations and landers. The software factory lives with the mission and keeps onboard and edge modules improving under governance. Sell the ground first and earn the right to fly.": "audio/v_c85b6941bb95510a.mp3",
"Where to sell first. 1,444 prospects in the United States, the first market, and 3,141 in Europe, served in parallel through the German entity. Each dot on the globe is one of them; open a play for its list.": "audio/v_28912390a6087308.mp3",
"What we do not claim. Terrarium does not generate flight software today; onboard plays are a destination and need flight qualification. Not certified; a funded certification path follows the close. No space revenue yet; the prospect counts are census companies that match a play, not a pipeline. No market size is claimed for any play; the dollar figures shown are the atlas cells' own cited numbers.": "audio/v_f29e67b22793909e.mp3",
